      <PrimaryExemptPurposeTxt>TO IMPROVE HEALTH CARE FOR VULNERABLE CHILDREN AND YOUNG ADULTS LIVING IN SHELTERS.</PrimaryExemptPurposeTxt>
      <ProgramSrvcAccomplishmentGrp>
        <DescriptionProgramSrvcAccomTxt>For over two decades, Bridges to Healing International has provided essential healthcare to more than 7,000 vulnerable youth in shelters across three countries. As a grassroots organization, we act swiftly and efficiently, making informed decisions to deliver critical healthcare resources where they are needed most. Through close collaboration with local partners, we ensure personalized care shaped by thousands of hours spent in shelters. Our approach focuses on achievable, tailored solutions that meet the unique needs of vulnerable young people. Our short-term goal is to improve daily well-being, while our long-term vision is to empower youth to lead fulfilling, independent lives. We serve as a vital bridge between institutional care and local medical resources, ensuring comprehensive physical and mental healthcare. In recent years, we have increased our focus on adolescent womenone of the most vulnerable populations in institutional care.Key Accomplishments in 2024:- Expanded healthcare services to over 400 high-risk children and young women inorphanages and institutional care settings in Bolivia and Tanzania.- Funded healthcare costs in youth shelters, including full-time nurse salaries,medications, lab tests, surgeries, and physical therapy.- Launched specialized health programs for under-resourced young women,particularly those who are disabled, Indigenous, or survivors of gender-basedviolence. These initiatives empower young women with access to comprehensivehealthcare, promote well-being, and reduce reliance on institutional care.- Established Tanzanias first audiology testing program for hearing-impaired youthliving in shelters, addressing a critical shortage of audiologists.- Expanded efforts to reduce institutionalization by funding community health workers who provide physical and mental health support to single mothers raising children with chronic illnesses and disabilities, helping prevent unnecessary institutionalization.- Provided social work, infectious disease, and healthcare consultations to improve sanitation and long-term health outcomes for youth in shelters where we operate.</DescriptionProgramSrvcAccomTxt>
